  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Go to any site on a Friday afternoon. Someone is filling in a form about Monday. They are not lying. They are remembering. That is not the same thing.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The argument, briefly",
      "html": "<ul><li>Every record has a gap between what was seen and what was written.</li><li>Memory fills that gap. Memory is not evidence.</li><li>The problem is not lying. Detail fades.</li><li>A record made on the day, with a dated photo, is easier to trust.</li><li>Quickler records on the spot, so you do not write from memory.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The gap",
      "heading": "Between the floor and the desk",
      "html": "<p>You walk a building at nine. You see forty things. Most are fine. A few are not.</p><p>You cannot stop to write each one down. So you write them up later. By then the small details have gone.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The tools",
      "heading": "Paper and apps both wait for the desk",
      "html": "<p>A better form does not fix this. Paper is filled in later at the office. An app is often opened later too.</p><p>The problem is when you record. Not what you record on.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "What a reader sees",
      "heading": "You can see the difference",
      "html": "<p>Inspectors are trained to spot it. The regulators differ: the Care Quality Commission in England, the Care Inspectorate in Scotland, Care Inspectorate Wales, the RQIA in Northern Ireland and HIQA in Ireland. They ask the same question. Does this service really know itself?</p><p>Records made on the day, with dated photos and the observer's own words, look honest. A neat folder, all dated the same afternoon, does not.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The fix",
      "heading": "Record where you see it",
      "html": "<p>Record at the moment you look. Not later. Not at the desk.</p><p>In Quickler you answer in WhatsApp as you work, by voice note, text or photo. When you say done, Quickler writes the report. It never makes things up. Every name and number comes from something you said, sent or photographed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>Is a record written later a problem if it is accurate?</h3><p>You cannot show that it is accurate. A record rebuilt from memory loses detail you cannot see. It has no timestamp or photo to back it up. A regulator or insurer may ask for proof.</p><h3>Can inspectors tell the difference?</h3><p>They are trained to. A folder of entries all done at the same time looks different from records made across a week, each dated to what was seen.</p><h3>How does recording on the day help?</h3><p>The record comes from what you saw, not what you recall. With Quickler you answer in WhatsApp as you work. There is no app or login, so there is no reason to leave it until the end of the day.</p><h3>Is Quickler a care records system?</h3><p>No. It is not a care records system, and it is not for clinical records. It records inspections, audits and the evidence behind them. Anything clinical stays in your care system.</p><h3>Related reading</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/what-is-quickler.html\">What is Quickler</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-compliance-software-uk.html\">Care home compliance software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-inspectorate-compliance-software-scotland.html\">Care Inspectorate compliance software (Scotland)</a></li></ul>"
    }
